height="264" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="42" width="373" WOHratio="1.4158415841584158" y="1134" link="" x="132" uniqueID="element105" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="1.4158415841584158" imageRatio="1.4158415841584158" editMode="normalMode" origWidth="429" origHeight="303" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="1169" name="Text-58" x="565" templateID="" width="381" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="228" uniqueID="element107"><Paragraph><Segment color="#000000" font-size="12" font-family="Helvetica" font-style="bold"> Looking at these clay tablets provides a wondrous glance into the importance of clay during this period of history. Not only did clay provide plates to eat upon and urns from which to drink wine, it provided the substance that gave us the written word. On the pottery produced in this area we see carved and painted strokes that relate to these angular “signs”, providing a decorative element that is typical of the pottery of the Middle East. </Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element108" deletable="" border-width="0" y="1401" x="185" width="259" editable="true" name="Text-59" height="32" padding="0" groupID="42" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment color="#FFFFFF">Babylon, Clay Tablets with Cuneiform text,</Segment></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment color="#FFFFFF">1,500-1,400 BPE,   7 1/8 x 3  5/8 in.</Segment></Paragr